{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact F head acdb7ec23979b75dcae5b4addfc4b637afaaefde on builder/canonical-spelling-f-20261110, all 82 artifacts, for request 2ad35350 under promise 226d9000 and review request 93a28c0d. New authored definitions now have one spelling. The kernel owns the rule, and authoring refuses any other spelling. Every recorded definition, journal and signature keeps its bytes, and every reader that knew an old Change pin still accepts it.\n\nWhat I checked in the source:\n\n- The kernel rule. `normalizeDefinition` in `packages/derive/src/validate/spelling.ts` replaces the copy in `packages/define`. A nested guard drops `of` only when `of` equals its enclosing guard's subject. That matches `guardBody` in `validate/guards.ts:96-98` and `nested` at line 140, which pass the enclosing subject to each child (section 6.5). The old Define copy dropped only `of: \"on\"`. `has.where` stays because the validator requires it, and an empty `each.where` goes. For `fact.where`, only `b` reads the guard's subject, and only `b` changes.\n- Canonical mode. `validateDefinition` refuses only when `canonical: true`, and only after the definition passes. It compares `normalizeDefinition(declared)` with the input. `declared` is the input itself (`format-1.ts:167`). Only two callers pass the option: `compile` in Define and the CLI's author-file reader `application-files.ts:80`. Signed-package, journal, archive and Replay readers keep the default. The `canonical: true` options in `journal-*`, `archive-retained.ts` and `verify.ts` belong to `domainRetainedJSON` and predate this head.\n- Old pins stay readable. On main, the seven old Change pins appear in the lane digest tables, `publishing-provenance.ts`, Page `source-support.ts` and `evidence-lane-pins.generated.ts`. At the head, each reader of a recorded lane still accepts its old pin:\n  - Page keeps both LIST1 pins and adds the two current ones.\n  - The views keep the recorded J4 and Finding Change pins beside the current ones.\n  - `evidenceIncidentParentV17` accepts exactly the recorded and current Change4 pins. The Directory `open-pr` must name the same pin as the parent genesis seed, so a mixed pair fails.\n  - `PUBLISHING_LANE_DIGESTS` has no production reader. Only tests, `pin.mjs` and the platform index name it.\n  - Clients open a lane under the definition that the room's rules scope holds active (`command-change.ts:92`, `retained-editor-data.ts:103`). A live room keeps creating lanes under its old pin.\n- The editors. The CLI `supportsEdit` and the Page `supports` accept `{ slot: \"rulesScope\", of: \"on\" }` and `{ slot: \"rulesScope\" }`, and no other spelling. The Editor coverage hash and the seal changes in `security-entry-points.test.mjs` follow those two functions. The three new seals cover `validate/index.ts`, `spelling.ts` and `evidence-incident-origin-v17.ts`.\n- Grow. `room.ts`, `device.ts` and `demo-definitions.ts` now omit `of: \"on\"` at source. `prepare-check.ts` rewrites only the copied fault scenario to the fresh Room digest. The tracked template, the signed demo packet and the retained TEST declarations keep their bytes.\n\nMy probes, run on my own extracted copy of the head with no shared validation slot:\n\n- For all 13 lane definitions, `normalizeDefinition` of the main bytes equals the head bytes. Each head definition passes canonical validation. The seven changed Change definitions fail canonical validation at their main bytes. Their compiled forms, without paths, digest and declared bytes, equal the head's. The digests match the head's tables: change `bdeba17b`, change-demo `91d40909`, change3 `dd77737e`, change-demo3 `36529a73`, change-evidence4 `ea0a57d0`, change-finding `16b1ff90` and change-j4 `e2765ff4`. The six issue definitions keep their pins.\n- The retained Grow Room refuses canonical mode and passes the default mode at `5e2d34fa`. The retained Grow Site passes both modes at `69424c1b`. The note says the same of each.\n- I wrapped a guard of `authorize-export` in an `anyOf` whose two alternatives repeat the enclosing `of: \"also.hold\"`. The default mode admits it, and canonical mode refuses it. Its normalized form passes canonical mode and compiles to the same program.\n\nEvidence and digests:\n\n- Scope. Main d7736fa3 is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 82 paths that its 82 artifacts cover. The commit-message check passes with 30 checked and 0 flagged, `git diff --check` is clean, and `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. On my extracted copy, `check-doc-links.mjs` checks 1,216 local links and finds none broken.\n- The follow-up. From gate head 688cda1e, tree be8a8e57, the head changes only four reporting paths: the primary note, the new history annex and the two final manifests.\n- The manifests. Each of the 24 manifest copies hashes to the prefix that the note's table cites. The final gate manifest hashes to 778d014b…0682, and the static manifest hashes to 58d18151…b4749, as the review request states. The three unit files match their recorded SHA-256 values, sizes and mode 0400, and `SHA256SUMS` verifies. The private manifest hashes to 1b20b7e8…279b, as recorded.\n- The gate. `gate.exit` and `driver.exit` read \"exit 0\". The receipt records head 688cda1e, exit 0 and an unchanged source, and its tree reads be8a8e57. The Native log ends with 290 files passed and one skipped, and 1,134 tests passed with two skipped. The log contains no SIGBUS, heap abort or Worker exit.\n- The browser. GitHub reports Page run 42893173451 and Card run 42895299526 as attempt 1, completed and successful, at 3de5bb68. Between 3de5bb68 and 688cda1e, only Markdown, three test files and the two retained TEST declarations change. None of them is Page or Card source.\n- The history. The seven failed runs and the failed gate at 43e9b328 keep their failed outcomes in their own manifests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Definition identity joins the runtime and Replay, so the spelling rule belongs in the kernel under section 7, and one copy now serves Define, the CLI and the tests. The head enforces the rule only where an author writes. Recorded bytes keep their identity, and the identifiers cutover (c+d) can turn the rule on at new-format fold boundaries without a second algorithm. The relocation also fixes the old Define copy, which dropped only `of: \"on\"` and ignored a nested guard's inherited subject.\n\nSecurity: no finding. Canonical mode only refuses. It never rewrites bytes before a signature or a digest. The Change4 parent check widens from one exact pin to two exact pins, both real Change4 identities. It also now requires the Directory `open-pr` definition to equal the parent seed, which is stricter than before. Every reader of a recorded lane still accepts its pin, so the deployment strands no live lane. The new seals pin the boundary files.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Three notes:\n\n- The refusal names no path: \"must omit redundant subjects and empty optional filters in canonical mode\". Define authors never see it, because `compile` normalizes first. A CLI author with a hand-written JSON file gets no location. A later head can report the first differing path.\n- An empty `has.guards` list survives normalization, while an empty `each.where` does not. Both read the same. Canonical spelling is unique for the forms the rule covers, not for every optional list.\n- The primary note links seven proofs and readbacks under `/private/tmp`, including the closure plan for the 703/727/398 inputs. They exist now, but a restart erases them. The repository manifests and the sealed custody under `artroom-evidence` remain the durable record.\n\nMigration and rollback: the head adds no Durable Object migration and changes no `wrangler` file or setting. Deployment changes the Page bundle and the definitions that new Grow rooms, new lane activations and the examples use. A rollback means restoring the earlier Worker versions. Rooms created after the deployment keep the new pins, and the earlier readers do not know those pins. The `examples/repo-in-ai` loop opens and reads J4 lanes only under the current pin. Against a room whose rules activated the old J4 Change, it needs the new definition activated first. The planner decides the deployment, any reactivation and the hosted checks. This approval authorizes no deployment, publication, live acceptance or J5 claim.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a400e6e31a29b7ee94653ff3d7d410dea0e6f11f","binding":"assigned","head":"acdb7ec23979b75dcae5b4addfc4b637afaaefde","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5ae87289b67f685cb61b594c0bdce992c2937b26\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:226d90000f49ddd6c0b93d5a0b6fd791cd5814d2\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5ae87289b67f685cb61b594c0bdce992c2937b26","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}